<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Foreign Legion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Foreign Legion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> is a branch of the French army founded in 1831 for foreign volunteers. It served in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and in many other colonial campaigns, becoming one of the best-known military instruments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion.jpg" alt="French Foreign Legion" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Infographie Képi blanc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grenade_legion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Louis Philippe, King of the French, created the Foreign Legion on 10 March 1831 to bring foreign nationals into the French Army from the foreign regiments of the old kingdom. Its recruits included soldiers from the recently disbanded Swiss and German regiments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy. The royal ordinance allowed the foreigners to serve only outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The French expeditionary force that had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1830 needed reinforcements, so the Legion was sent by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. It was first divided into six national battalions: Swiss, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Germans, Italians, Spanish and Dutch-Belgian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> was initially stationed only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, where it took part in the conquest, described as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and in the drying of marshes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. It served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1835, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> in 1854 and the Italian campaign of 1859. It was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico">French intervention in Mexico</a> from 1863 to 1867 and the Tonkin campaign and Sino-French War of the 1880s. French law kept it out of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> except in an invasion, so it was not in the army that capitulated at Sedan in 1870.</p><p>It helped extend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. After 1871 volunteers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> joined it, and it fought in many critical battles on the Western Front in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Its numbers swelled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, and it suffered heavy losses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a>, which fell on 7 May 1954.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a> regiments fought the National Liberation Front. By the mid-1960s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> had lost its home in French Algeria, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, who considered disbanding it, cut it from 40,000 to 8,000 men and moved it to metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Over its history it has lost nearly 40,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> is the only unit of the French Army open to people of any nationality, and most of its commissioned officers are French, with about 10 per cent promoted from the ranks. Legionnaires may apply for French citizenship after three years' service, or at once if wounded in the line of duty, a provision known as Français par le sang versé. Its flags bear the motto Honneur et Fidélité rather than Honneur et Patrie. It marches at 88 steps a minute, against 116 for other French units, and always comes last in a parade. Its marching song is Le Boudin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Foreign Legion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>French Foreign Legion</b> was founded in 1831 for service in Algeria and kept its headquarters at Sidi Bel Abbès until Algerian independence in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The second empire relied heavily on the <b>Foreign Legion</b>, founded in 1831, and the tirailleurs, colonial infantry recruited from Senegal, Algeria, Indochina, and elsewhere, who fought in both world wars and whose pay and pensions lagged far behind those of their French counterparts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>French rule rested on a colonial army that combined French regulars, the <b>Foreign Legion</b> and North African and Senegalese units with large numbers of Indochinese riflemen (tirailleurs) recruited locally.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>French Foreign Legion</b> adopted it as early as the 19th century, and the Chilean Armed Forces and the National Army of Colombia also use it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</a></li></ul></section>
